use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging
from ../Dialog import Dialog

export ui-component Drawer:
    description:
        A drawer is a panel that slides in from an edge of the screen, such as
        a sheet rising from the bottom on a phone or a side panel of filters.
        It is a dialog in every other way. On touch screens the user can drag
        it to resize it or swipe it away.

    composes:
        - @{Dialog}

    behaviors:
        - @{Dragging}

    edge:
        The component says which edge it comes from. For right-to-left
        languages, the start and end sides swap. It slides in and out from
        that edge.

    blocking:
        It blocks the page like a Dialog by default. The component can make it
        non-modal, such as a side panel that stays open while the user works
        on the page.

    snapPoints:
        The component can give it several resting sizes, such as half the
        screen and the full screen. Dragging it lets go at the nearest one, or
        the next one along if the user flicks it. Whether a flick can skip past
        a size is up to the component. The component is told which size it
        rests at, and can set it.

    swipeToClose:
        Dragging it back towards its edge past a certain point, or flicking it
        that way quickly, closes it. The component can turn this off. Dragging
        it only starts from the drawer itself or a grab handle, and a
        scrolling list inside it scrolls rather than moving the drawer until
        the list reaches its end.

    swipeToOpen:
        The component can let the user open it by swiping in from its edge of
        the screen.

    keyboard:
        Dragging is never the only way to close it. Escape and a close button
        work as they do in a Dialog. Whether the user can move between resting
        sizes from the keyboard, such as with a grab handle that takes focus,
        is up to the chosen library; where they can't, the drawer opens at a
        size that shows everything it needs to.

    stacking:
        A drawer can open another on top of it. Whether the ones underneath
        shrink back or dim to show the stack is up to the chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Not a permanent side bar. A panel that is always part of the layout
            isn't a drawer
        - Not attached to anything on the page. That's a Popover
